// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Without it the checker cannot find the virtual
// environment, so every import of app.* and tools.* is reported unresolved. That
// produced dozens of false errors on correct code, and a checker that is wrong
// that often is one people stop reading. Run properly it found three real
// problems in app/, all fixed: a plan.py guard whose non-None invariant was set
// eighty lines away, and a quarter label typed as str on both sides of the API
// boundary.
//
// SCOPE IS app/ ONLY, and the boundary is a measurement rather than a
// preference. With the same settings app/ reports 0 errors, tools/ reports 105
// and tests/ 194 - and every one of those 299 is the same untypeable pattern: a
// fixture or a params preset held as a dict and splatted, as in
// SupplyDemandParams(**POPULATION) where POPULATION infers dict[str, float] so
// each int, bool and Literal field complains. There is no runtime defect in any
// of them and no fix that does not damage the fixtures. Gating the shipped code
// at zero is worth having; gating it at 299 known-false is worth nothing.
//
// COMMENTS ARE `//` LINES, not `"//"` KEYS. The two paragraphs above were string
// values under the keys "//" and "//scope", which is the usual JSON
// comment-smuggling trick - and pyright reads this file as JSONC, so it parsed
// them as real settings and answered "Config contains unrecognized setting" for
// each one on every run. Two warnings printed beside every clean result, which
// is how a clean result stops being read.
{
  "venvPath": ".",
  "venv": ".venv",
  "include": ["app"],
  "exclude": [".venv", "**/__pycache__", ".cache", ".pytest_cache"],
  "typeCheckingMode": "standard",
  "reportMissingImports": "error",
  "pythonVersion": "3.13",
  "pythonPlatform": "Windows"
}
